Word-sign model training data: Google Isolated Sign Language Recognition (Kaggle "asl-signs")

Google, "Google - Isolated Sign Language Recognition", Kaggle competition data (2023),
https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/asl-signs. License: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0),
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. The data was created for PopSign (https://www.popsign.org/), a project
of the Center on Access Technology at the Rochester Institute of Technology's National Technical Institute for the
Deaf (NTID) with Google and the Georgia Institute of Technology; the signs were collected by the Deaf Professional
Arts Network. It is MediaPipe Holistic landmark sequences (no images or video) of 250 ASL signs by 21 signers in the
released training set.

Changes by Handspell: from the landmark sequences of 36 signs (the 12 free words in `classifier/words-v3.bin` and the 24
Pro words in `classifier/words-pro-v3.bin`) plus about 50 unrelated signs used as an "other" class, Handspell kept only
the dominant-hand, nose and shoulder points, computed its own features, and trained the two word-recognition networks.
The example animations in `content/word-refs.json` are drawn from individual sequences of that data, shown as hand
wireframes with a generic face outline. The landmark files are not bundled with the app. No endorsement by Google,
PopSign, NTID, Georgia Tech, the Deaf Professional Arts Network or the signers is implied. The trained weights and the
example animations are supplied under CC BY 4.0, independently of the application code's MIT license.
